  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 102 AM MDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nFriday - Sunday: Heading into the end of the week and through the \nweekend, a brief pattern change will start to take place. \nSynoptically, southwesterly flow will still be in place through \nFriday, though moisture content will start to lessen. Then heading \nthrough the weekend, ridging develops over the area. For Friday, \nisolated to scattered showers and storms are anticipated given the \nflow pattern in place, and at least some moisture remaining. This \nprecipitation is expected to favor the mountains though, where \norographic forcing will be ongoing. Heading through the weekend, \ndryer conditions then prevail as moisture lessens further, and \nsubsidence increases. With that said, showers may start to increase \nin coverage along the Continental Divide late Sunday night ahead of \nan approaching trough. Otherwise, partly to mostly cloudy skies \nFriday will become mostly clear to partly cloudy through the \nweekend as the drier air arrives. In addition, winds will remain \nrelatively light through this period, though may be breezier across \nthe far southeastern plains Friday afternoon. Looking at \ntemperatures, a warming trend starts Friday and continues through \ninto Sunday. Friday will hover around seasonal values, with much of \nthe region warming to above seasonal values by Sunday. \n\nMonday - Wednesday: Then for the start of next week, active weather \nmakes a return to south central and southeastern Colorado. As we \nhead into next week, troughing is expected to impact the area. This \npattern will bring elevated precipitation chances, particularly \nMonday and Tuesday, and cooler temperatures for much of the area. \nPrecipitation is anticipated to favor the mountains though, where \nforcing will be greatest. And for those wondering, yes that does \nmean snow potential for the mountains. Confidence does remain medium \nto high (50-70%) in this pattern, with continued agreement between \nensemble guidance. With that all said, uncertainty does still \nremain, such as amplification of the trough, how much moisture is \npulled northward, and could there be influences from the tropical \nremnants/moisture of ongoing hurricane Polo? Along with that, clouds \nand winds would be expected to start increasing as the unsettled \npattern is in place. As for temperatures, Monday is anticipated to \nbe the warmest day ahead of the troughing, with Tuesday and \nWednesday cooling down to below seasonal values behind the system \nand likely associated cold front.",
